{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Let it not seem hard in your eyes when you send him free</b> — and with the gift <b>from your flock.</b> <b>For double the wage of a hired worker</b> — for indeed the term of a hired worker is three years, as it is written: 'three years, like the years of a hired worker,' and this one, a slave, [serves] six years. Another interpretation: construe it thus — <b>כי משנה</b> — for double: six years your slave served, meaning twice three years, which are six years. For the verse speaks of the boring-through (נרצע), and the final term of servitude is not precisely six years, but sometimes less and sometimes more." "<b>HaShem your Elohim will bless you.</b> In every place where HaShem tells Israel to give up of their own for any commandment, there He promises them that they will not lose by it, for He will bless them in the work of their hands."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The text reads 'ונקלה כבוד מואב' (Isaiah 16:14), apparently quoting the phrase about 'three years like a hired worker'; the words appear to be an alternate scriptural citation or gloss that entered the main text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:52:59.865410713Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}